"""Render exact calibration measurements and their scoped interpretation."""
import argparse,json,base64,html,math
from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

p=argparse.ArgumentParser();p.add_argument('--evidence-dir',type=Path,required=True);p.add_argument('--output',type=Path,required=True);a=p.parse_args();out=a.output
r=json.loads((a.evidence_dir/'record.json').read_text());audit=json.loads((out/'assessment.json').read_text());keys=['evolution_observed_error','observer_baseline_error','total_observed_error'];labels=['Evolution','Interpolation baseline','Total'];budget=r['config']['measurement_error_budget'];anchors=r['anchors']
fig,axes=plt.subplots(2,2,figsize=(12,8),layout='constrained');colors=['#226498','#bc6410','#528151']
for field,color in [('hopfion',colors[0]),('packet',colors[1])]:
 rows=[v for v in anchors if v['parameters']['field']==field];xs=[v['parameters']['nodes'] for v in rows];ys=[100*v['reference'][keys[0]] for v in rows]
 axes[0,0].plot(xs,ys,'o-',label=field.title(),color=color)
 axes[0,0].plot(xs,ys[0]*(xs[0]/np.array(xs))**2,'--',color=color,alpha=.5,label='N⁻² reference' if field=='hopfion' else None)
axes[0,0].set(yscale='log',xlabel='Native nodes N (saved physical states)',ylabel='Cell-aware evolution error (%)',title='Spatial trend remains close to second order');axes[0,0].legend()
names=[v['parameters']['field'].title()+'\nN'+str(v['parameters']['nodes']) for v in anchors];xs=np.arange(6)
axes[0,1].bar(xs,[v['original33_max_fraction']/budget for v in audit['anchor_results']],color=[colors[0]]*3+[colors[1]]*3)
axes[0,1].axhline(1,color='black',ls='--');axes[0,1].set_xticks(xs,names,fontsize=8);axes[0,1].set(yscale='log',ylabel='Largest norm difference / 1e-6 budget',title='Original 33-node observer needs qualification')
v=anchors[2]
for key,label,color in zip(keys,labels,colors):
 rows=sorted(v['development']+v['validation'],key=lambda x:x['observer_nodes'])
 axes[1,0].plot([w['observer_nodes'] for w in rows],[max(abs(w[key]-v['reference'][key]),1e-16)/budget for w in rows],'o-',label=label,color=color,ms=4)
axes[1,0].axhline(1,color='black',ls='--');axes[1,0].set(yscale='log',xlabel='Observer nodes M',ylabel='Norm difference / 1e-6 budget',title='Hopfion N129: higher M can worsen quadrature');axes[1,0].legend(fontsize=8)
for offset,key,label,color in zip([-.25,0,.25],keys,labels,colors):
 values=[v['models'][key]['models'][0]['validation_max_abs_error']/budget for v in anchors];axes[1,1].bar(xs+offset,values,width=.23,label=label,color=color)
axes[1,1].axhline(1,color='black',ls='--');axes[1,1].set_xticks(xs,names,fontsize=8);axes[1,1].set(yscale='log',ylabel='Fresh prediction residual / 1e-6 budget',title='Prediction failure is separate from integration accuracy');axes[1,1].legend(fontsize=8)
for ax in axes.flat:ax.grid(alpha=.2)
fig.suptitle('Saved-state observer calibration — six anchors, retained qualifications',fontsize=15);fig.savefig(out/'assessment-plots.png',dpi=300);plt.close(fig)
yes=lambda b:'Pass' if b else 'Open / fail'
table=''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in [v['field'].title(),v['native_nodes'],yes(v['cell_reference_pass']),yes(v['uniform_pass']),yes(v['prediction_pass'])])+'</tr>' for v in audit['anchor_results'])
evolution=''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in [v['parameters']['field'].title(),v['parameters']['nodes'],f"{100*v['development'][0][keys[0]]:.8f}",f"{100*v['reference'][keys[0]]:.8f}",f"{v['reference'][keys[0]]-v['development'][0][keys[0]]:+.8g}"])+'</tr>' for v in anchors)
img=base64.b64encode((out/'assessment-plots.png').read_bytes()).decode()
body='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Matter Waves — observer calibration assessment</title><style>body{font:16px/1.65 system-ui;max-width:1200px;padding:20px;margin:auto;color:#19334a}h1,h2{line-height:1.25}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px}td,th{padding:8px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;text-align:left}img{max-width:100%}li{margin-bottom:10px}code{overflow-wrap:anywhere}.scope{background:#f0f4f6;padding:14px}</style><h1>Observer calibration: outcome assessment</h1><p><strong>Disposition: useful measurement diagnosis, with partial qualification.</strong> The saved fields show real observer-resolution sensitivity, particularly in interpolation and total-error norms. Cell-aware integration gives a stable evolution-error measurement at all six anchors. The registered all-response checks remain mixed: five cell references pass, two uniform observers pass, and only one anchor passes every fresh curve-prediction check. Original failures and thresholds remain unchanged.</p><p>Assessment timestamps: '''+html.escape(json.dumps(audit['assessment_at']))+'''. The workstation ran from 14:01:49 to 14:19:05 Australia/Melbourne on 14 November 2026 (03:01:49–03:19:05 UTC), approximately 17 min 15 s.</p><h2>Integrity, execution and scope</h2><p>All 209 ZIP entries pass CRC and all 208 declared compact hashes match. There are 82 norm evaluations: 40 development observer resolutions, 18 reserved observer validations, 18 cell-Gauss measurements and six midpoint controls. No new physical-time integration ran. All six anchor records match their sealed measurement receipts. Code hashes match the identity record. Selections precede their measurements, validation counts are disjoint, and all frozen fits precede validation. I independently recalculated every candidate validation residual, the squared-norm decomposition and the registered readiness decisions.</p><p class="scope">The six underlying NPZ fields are absent from this compact archive. Their recorded identities are retained, but this review does not independently hash or replay their bytes. The recorded checkout commit is <code>'''+html.escape(r['environment']['source_commit'])+'''</code>; it is provenance, not live GitHub/release verification. The source 35-state run remains <code>20261114t000026z-1620e2b1</code>.</p><h2>Registered outcomes</h2><table><tr><th>Field</th><th>Native grid N</th><th>Cell reference, all norms</th><th>Uniform readiness</th><th>Fresh prediction, all norms</th></tr>'''+table+'''</table><p>The budget is 1e-6 as a fractional field norm (0.0001 percentage points), one fifth of the earlier physical-mesh prediction limit. Uniform readiness requires a passing cell reference, two consecutive qualifying resolution additions, and a final independent midpoint comparison. A curve prediction is a different requirement: an accurate selected quadrature does not guarantee that a smooth empirical curve predicts every lower-resolution observer.</p><img src="data:image/png;base64,'''+img+'''" alt="Measured convergence, observer shifts and frozen-model validation residuals"><h2>What is now established</h2><p><strong>The primary evolution-error metric has stable cell-aware integration.</strong> Gauss orders 3→4 change this metric by at most 8.14e-13 across the six states, far below the 1e-6 measurement budget. This follows the expected structure: on each native interpolation cell the evolution-vector component is trilinear, and its square is integrated exactly by a tensor two-point Gauss rule in exact arithmetic. The exact-field denominator still needs its own numerical convergence check; its recorded values are consistent across native meshes to about 3.4e-13 relative for Hopfion and 1.1e-14 for the packet at the higher order. This supports empirical integration accuracy, not a rigorous universal error bound.</p><table><tr><th>Field</th><th>N</th><th>Original evolution error (%)</th><th>Cell-aware evolution error (%)</th><th>Change (fraction)</th></tr>'''+evolution+'''</table><p>The refined evolution errors still approach second-order spatial behaviour. Pairwise apparent powers over N49→81→129 are about 1.897 and 1.961 for Hopfion, and 1.991 and 1.997 for the packet. The physical spatial-refinement conclusion survives the measurement correction. These sparse pairwise powers are descriptive; this calibration has not supplied a new all-mesh validated physical relationship.</p><p><strong>The largest observer problems concern interpolation and total error.</strong> For Hopfion N129, changing from the 33-node observer to the order-4 cell-aware reference raises total error from 0.594562% to 0.606648%, and raises the interpolation baseline by 4.33% relative. Packet N129 total error changes from 0.0559683% to 0.0580434%, while its baseline rises by 6.35% relative. These are changes in the measurement of the same physical fields, not better or worse physical trajectories.</p><p>At Hopfion N81, the evolution metric changes by +4.3835e-6 as a fraction, comparable with the earlier 5e-6 mesh-fit tolerance. The original Hopfion physical-mesh prediction failure therefore remains sensitive to the observation contract. It is not legitimate to correct only that withheld point or call the old failed fit a pass: development meshes must be remeasured consistently, and the earlier failure retained.</p><h2>Why a larger uniform observer is insufficient</h2><p>Hopfion N129 illustrates the problem directly. At M160 and M224 the largest differences from the cell-aware reference are only 1.88e-7 and 1.58e-7, respectively. At M257 the largest difference worsens to 1.36e-5. Its midpoint control also differs by 2.70e-5. Packet N49 has an analogous excursion at M197 after much smaller differences at M117/M164. This is deterministic sampling sensitivity of fixed arrays, consistent with observer/native-cell commensurability; the evidence does not establish a unique aliasing mechanism or a physical bifurcation. Brute-force enlargement of M and a single smooth polynomial/exponential in observer spacing are unreliable general remedies.</p><p>The six observer curves are mostly failing on baseline/total predictions. Five of six evolution-only primary fits pass their reserved observer predictions, but only packet N81 passes all three responses. Packet N129 is an instructive separation: its selected M192 observer passes readiness, while its baseline/total prediction curves fail. Do not merge those distinct outcomes or switch the primary model retrospectively after seeing validation.</p><h2>Remaining qualifications by state</h2><ul><li><strong>Hopfion N49:</strong> order-3→4 baseline change is 2.004e-6 and total change 1.156e-6, so the joint reference fails. Its evolution change is only 8.13e-13. A focused order-5/6 check on this same state is the appropriate way to test the unresolved analytical/interpolation quadrature.</li><li><strong>Hopfion N81:</strong> final M257 and midpoint comparisons individually meet 1e-6, but there were no two consecutive qualifying adaptive rounds. The formal failure remains; the final point alone cannot rewrite the stopping rule.</li><li><strong>Hopfion N129:</strong> the large M257 reversal and midpoint disagreement are substantive failures of the uniform measurement, despite accurate cell-aware results.</li><li><strong>Packet N49:</strong> final M257 and midpoint comparisons pass individually, but the preceding excursion breaks the consecutive-round certificate. The aggregate unresolved status is correct.</li><li><strong>Packet N81:</strong> M141 passes both the formal readiness and all-response fresh prediction checks.</li><li><strong>Packet N129:</strong> M192 passes readiness; the all-response prediction check still fails, primarily in the interpolation baseline.</li></ul><p>The native norm budget/observer cap was respected. Several runs reached M257 before using all four allowed additions because count selection is strictly increasing. “Unresolved” therefore sometimes reflects the resolution cap or the consecutive criterion, rather than an exhausted count of permitted additions. No rule is relaxed retrospectively.</p><h2>Recommended next work</h2><ol><li>Record this calibration and the partial-success assessment as new immutable evidence through the workstation. Preserve the earlier 35-state measurement and failed fits.</li><li>Use targeted cell-aware confirmation: Gauss orders 5 and 6 for Hopfion N49, and a higher-order anchor check if adopting one common all-response instrument. Keep physical fields, cube and interpolation fixed.</li><li>Once that observation contract is adequate, remeasure all existing spacing states — sixteen Hopfion and nine packet meshes — with the same cell-aware rule. No new physical evolution is needed for that task. Keep all original 33-node values alongside the revised measurements.</li><li>Use these consistent measurements for descriptive dependency fits. Freeze any revised predictive model before a small newly reserved physical-mesh validation set; already inspected physical validation points are not fresh. Do not automatically choose the largest mesh or replace the old failure.</li><li>Proceed separately with the earlier Q2 evidence inventory and preparation of one controlled Q3 dependency. Maxwell observation results do not transfer numerical orders or tolerances into SF acceptance. Q1 remains parked; Q4/Q7 admission/preparation and the separate duration confirmation stay unresolved.</li></ol><p>No new large physical campaign is justified by these calibration failures alone. This assessment changes no source run, source equation, physical gate, full field array or GitHub state. It is an AI-assisted compact-evidence analysis, not independent scientific peer review or physical-model acceptance.</p></html>'''
(out/'ASSESSMENT.html').write_text(body)
print(json.dumps({'report':str(out/'ASSESSMENT.html'),'figure':str(out/'assessment-plots.png')}))
